  "reason": "KB-029: user entered password on phishing site (payroll verification lure, lookalike domain brightIine-help.example); treat password as stolen. Deliver temp password by phone after callback verification."
}

{
  "summary": "P1 credential compromise (KB-029). Reporter: Aisha Khan (user_id 5, HR Generalist, America/New_York, not VIP), device BL-LT-0105. She clicked a link in a 'payroll verification' email and typed her password on the site. Exact click time not given in the ticket; the matching message is probably Q-1003, 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain, capital I in place of l), received 2026-10-09 08:49 UTC. It is in quarantine marked phishing and cannot be released. Need to confirm whether she got a copy in her inbox or a different one. Entered: password. She did not say she approved a Duo push or opened an attachment. Sign-in logs (14 days): 9 sign-ins, all successful, all New York, US, on known residential/corporate IPs (198.51.100.25, 203.0.113.10) during her usual hours with Duo push. No hosting networks, no failures, no MFA denials. The latest is 2026-10-08 13:35 UTC, before the phishing email arrived, so no suspicious activity so far and block_sign_in was not requested. Duo: enrolled with Duo push on her phone. Action queued: reset_password (approval id 1). Temp password goes by phone after callback verification. Still needed: security review of the phishing message and the lookalike domain, a check of whether other users got it (HR/payroll lure), mailbox rule/forwarding review, keep watching sign-ins, and a block if anything unusual shows up.",
  "reason": "KB-029 says always escalate; ticket is P1."
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Aisha, thanks for reporting this so fast. You did the right thing. What happens next:\n\n1. We're treating the password you typed as stolen. A password reset has been requested and is waiting for a technician to approve it. Once it's approved, a technician will call you at the number on file to confirm it's you and give you a temporary password by phone. We will never send it by email. You'll set a new password the first time you sign in. Please don't reuse your old password anywhere, and change it on any other site where you used the same one.\n2. Please don't delete the email. Our security team may want it. Don't click it again or forward it to anyone.\n3. Never approve a Duo push you didn't start yourself. If you get one you didn't expect, deny it and let us know right away.\n\nWe checked your recent sign-ins and found nothing unusual so far. Your case has also gone to our senior security team, and they may contact you with follow-up questions. If you remember roughly what time you clicked the link, please reply with it.",
  "internal_note": "P1 phishing credential exposure, handled per KB-029 and KB-008. User: Aisha Khan (id 5), account active, Duo push enrolled. Her quarantine holds Q-1003, 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', from the lookalike domain brightIine-help.example, marked phishing and not releasable. It was received 2026-10-09 08:49 UTC and is probably the lure, but we need to confirm whether a copy reached her inbox. Sign-in logs: 9 successful sign-ins, all New York on known IPs during usual hours with Duo. No hosting networks, failures, or denials. The latest (10-08 13:35 UTC) is before the phish arrived, so block_sign_in was not requested. reset_password is queued (approval 1). After approval, deliver the temp password by phone after callback verification. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation 1) for security review, a check for other recipients, a mailbox rule/forwarding check, and continued sign-in monitoring. Block sign-in if anything suspicious appears.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-029",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
